        "title": "Explore Chunk in a classifier",
        "subtitle": "Request ordered parts and inspect how many actually exist.",
        "disclosureTitle": "How the sample directions work",
        "formula": "Request ordered parts and inspect how many actually exist.",
        "paragraphs": [
          "An array holds values in named directions. A sample is one observation; a batch groups observations. Sample direction selects where to cut each sample, keeping observations separate. A negative direction counts from the end.",
          "Requested parts is a request, not a promise. The block chooses equal widths rounded upward, so there may be fewer parts than requested. Thirteen positions divided with a request for six parts produce five parts; the last has one position. No empty parts are added.",
          "Use the actual part connectors in source order. Each connector exposes its own table and shape. After changing a setting, repair any connection to a part that no longer exists. The lesson asks for the final existing part and measures values, shape and input influence."
        ],
